From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 12:34:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08055/11362] A 461 MB row dump had stranded 411 commits on the
 canonical mirror for 8 days

Every push to github.com/Carnot-EBM/carnot-ebm failed from 2026-07-23 onward. GitHub's
pre-receive hook rejects a whole push if any commit in it carries a file over 100 MB, and
`results/experiment_5852_three_family_paired_embeddings.rows.jsonl` (439.8 MiB, added by the
conductor in 300a3a373) is in that range. gitea stayed current the entire time, so the two
mirrors silently diverged -- which is precisely what mirroring is supposed to prevent.

IT LOOKED LIKE ONE FAILING REMOTE, AND THAT IS WHY IT SURVIVED 8 DAYS. `origin` is a multi-URL
push remote (gitea AND github). `git push origin main` pushed both, gitea accepted, github
rejected, and the combined output read as a single error against one destination. Only
`git ls-remote` per URL shows the truth: gitea at HEAD, github 411 commits behind.

WHAT WAS DONE. `git filter-repo --path <path> --invert-paths --prune-empty never` on an ISOLATED
clone -- the canonical checkout was never touched while a measurement run was live in it.
Verified before publishing anything:

  commits           20753 -> 20753   (--prune-empty never; no commit dropped)
  files              32149 -> 32148   (diff is exactly the one removed path)
  surviving blobs   ALL 32148 sha-identical
  >100MB reachable  1 -> 0
  github HEAD       still an ancestor -> FAST-FORWARD, not a force-push

That last line is the one that mattered. Because the blob enters at 300a3a373 -- after github's
stale HEAD e02deec27 -- every commit github had already published kept its sha. No history
anyone else may have pulled was rewritten. Pushed e02deec27..6cb4b4623 cleanly.

THE FILE IS STILL ON DISK, AND I NEARLY LOST IT. `git checkout -B main <rewritten>` deleted it
from the working tree -- it was tracked in the old lineage and absent from the new one, so
checkout did exactly what it should. Restored from tag pre-filter-repo-backup-20260731 and
verified two ways: byte-identical to the original blob b224cfa0
(sha256 a76ccc5f...), and all 1944 rows re-hashed with the experiment's own `source_row_hash`
against their recorded values -- 0 mismatches. Anyone repeating this sequence should take the
backup tag FIRST; it is the only reason this was recoverable.

NOT A NEVER-PRUNE VIOLATION. Only bulk float data left git. The record survives in the tracked
637 KB companion .json -- claims, methodology, deterministic config hash, and per-row sha256
hashes for all 1944 rows -- plus the separate exp5853 integrity audit. Every row remains
individually attested and the corpus is regenerable from a pinned config.

THE .gitignore LINE IS LOAD-BEARING, not hygiene. The conductor and outer loop both run
`git add -A`; without it the file is re-added on the next commit and the mirror re-breaks
immediately.

DISCLOSED, NOT FIXED: a fresh clone no longer has this file, and three tracked code paths
reference it by name (experiment_5852.py:47, experiment_5862.py:123, and
test_experiment_5853...py:387). That matters for the G2 independent-reproducer gate, which runs
from a clean checkout. Left as a separate change on purpose -- altering test behaviour inside a
history rewrite would make both harder to review. ops/large-artifact-exclusions.md carries the
verification command, the recovery command, the regeneration path, and the note that Git LFS is
the better answer if this recurs.

The ~19 GB of other >100MB blobs (models/openai_privacy_filter/**) are NOT reachable from main --
they live on feature branches and the pre-filter-repo-backup-20260424 tag -- so they never
blocked this push and were left alone. Pushing those branches to GitHub will fail the same way.

